TITLE:: ServerPanel
summary:: a compact server window: counters, meters, scope, volume, recorder
categories:: Libraries>ServerPanel
related:: Classes/SPScope, Classes/SPFreqScope, Classes/SPMeter, Classes/SPTree, Classes/Server, Classes/Singleton

DESCRIPTION::
ServerPanel is a fork of Scott Carver's ServerView quark (link::https://github.com/scztt/ServerView.quark::), with its classes renamed so the two can be installed side by side. Both take over the window the server opens on boot, so keep one of them.

ServerPanel is a replacement for the standard SuperCollider server gui. It has a cleaner and more compact look, it supports multiple servers, and displays graphs of CPU usage, Synth counts, as well as a scope and input/output levels.

The window is drawn in a dark palette of its own, in the system sans-serif face, so it looks the same on every machine and the same as the BusRecorder and Insight windows it can open.

SECTION:: Keyboard shortcuts / interaction

TABLE::
## p || Open an link::Classes/SPTree::, the node tree as an outline
## n || Dump all node information to post window (code::s.queryAllNodes(true)::)
## l || Open an link::Classes/SPMeter::, the level meters
## b || Boot server (if not already running)
## s || Open an link::Classes/SPScope::, the oscilloscope in the same colours
## f || Open an link::Classes/SPFreqScope::, the frequency scope in the same colours
## i || Open an Insight window on the main out (needs the sc-insight extension; the code::i:: button by the meters does the same)
## r || Open the BusRecorder window (needs the sc-recorder extension; the code::r:: button in the record row does the same)
## x || Open the master fx chain - an eq and a compressor on the main out - in a window of its own, and close it again (needs the ParaEQ extension; the code::fx:: button under the counters does the same, and is lit while that window is up)
## d || Enable / disable dumping of OSC messages
## m || Mute server volume
## 0 || Reset server volume to 0dB
::

Horizontal / vertical mouse wheel or scroll actions over the scope will zoom horizontally and vertically.

Clicking graph widgets will reset their bounds.

Clicking the server name will boot / kill the server.


SECTION:: Adding widgets.

ServerPanel is populated by widgets that must be subclasses of link::Classes/SPWidgetBase::. To add a new widget type to the ServerPanel:

1. Create a subclass of link::Classes/SPWidgetBase::. It should not implement a constructor, and must implement a code::view:: method that returns a valid link::Classes/View::.

2. Add the class to link::#*widgets:: in your startup file, or any time before a ServerPanel is created.


subsection:: The stock windows

code::s.scope::, code::s.freqscope::, code::s.meter:: and code::s.plotTree:: - and the IDE's Server menu, which calls them - open link::Classes/SPScope::, link::Classes/SPFreqScope::, link::Classes/SPMeter:: and link::Classes/SPTree:: while link::#*replaceStockWindows:: is true.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: replaceStockWindows
Whether the server's own window methods open this quark's windows (true, the default) or the stock ones (false). Takes effect at once, no recompile.

METHOD:: new
Create a new ServerPanel

ARGUMENT:: key
A link::Classes/Symbol:: or other IdentityDictionary-appropriate object used to identify the ServerPanel. See link::Singleton#-new:: for behavior. Unless you need multiple ServerPanel's, no argument is required.

METHOD:: widgets

ARGUMENT:: widgetlist
A list of subclasses of SPWidgetBase to be displayed in the ServerPanel. By default, it contains SPStatusWidget, SPVolumeWidget, SPScopeWidget. Set this in your startup file to enable alternative configurations of widgets.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: close
Close the view;

COPYMETHOD:: View -onClose

METHOD:: front
Bring ServerPanel to front. If this ServerPanel has not been created yet, create it.

METHOD:: registerKeyAction
Add a keyboard action to the ServerPanel.

ARGUMENT:: key
A link::Classes/Char:: representing an ascii key.

ARGUMENT:: action
A function to execute when key is detected.


EXAMPLES::

ServerPanel works as a singleton, so the default constructor will always refer to the same object.
code::

ServerPanel().front;

ServerPanel().close;
::





